#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f202056-f0dd-5c9f-8d3c-c9a01c3abc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350793} {:answer/id #uuid "6097023d-a520-5f8e-a473-90715f679fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350794} {:answer/id #uuid "5419e692-07e7-5167-9ffc-27c417543520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350795} {:answer/id #uuid "7b96f3f4-3890-548f-bc99-28006851bb2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350796} {:answer/id #uuid "6571a62d-5572-54a6-bca7-9c8d2bb09225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5419e692-07e7-5167-9ffc-27c417543520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350795} :answer-field/id #uuid "de8f6a59-f658-5fa9-85d3-071ef7ea41c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91c4fe22-a2d9-5672-959d-b2b3675ff805" :question/math-academy-id "q-129783" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/779f39d9b8eca56cf8b7b2b9355364cd95171ca61c3e07cabd69e7a048933e22.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041d12b6-c95e-50e3-887f-75f323d28919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350800} {:answer/id #uuid "37a5d384-cda8-5db1-ba5d-5ce342db1ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350801} {:answer/id #uuid "753f5067-1d29-594c-96e7-7cee22bd8670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350802} {:answer/id #uuid "e04b7443-1b94-59f9-8b54-613545626f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350803} {:answer/id #uuid "68028880-9ac2-51a1-bc89-6d548a2368dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "041d12b6-c95e-50e3-887f-75f323d28919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350800} :answer-field/id #uuid "54de2e7b-2daf-56ed-90fd-68fe64149745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8d6c7cb-1514-5843-8dd1-d57ff5f9b373" :question/math-academy-id "q-129898" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/c319243db36af6829d83c8d5dfd7a724d884ff2506917249d324a315fd77ac28.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf89d997-8de3-5bd0-ba66-3a4d738d9124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350807} {:answer/id #uuid "04276af6-d649-548d-bebb-168f2ef49ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350808} {:answer/id #uuid "acd3ce3c-c2e9-5089-ace4-d3e77d41878e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350809} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c25953-c448-5ff7-9be3-dd9005faa768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350810} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a9c72f-c4f0-58df-91d8-6153fe647ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf89d997-8de3-5bd0-ba66-3a4d738d9124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350807} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea965f2-b652-516a-b4d3-c0df367f965d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fbf0c9f5-1d04-530e-9cd1-32b52a34d8d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129909" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ If $R=[-3,2]\\times [-2,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d144a0f2-0023-5663-a353-382e23eddd05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151387} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e25073-e8c8-572f-b7bb-7f9e85795377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151388} {:answer/id #uuid "0bda0c07-e5af-56dc-a66b-2597b288a22d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151389} {:answer/id #uuid "24216ea0-3609-56a2-ae45-4a2607173aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151390} {:answer/id #uuid "4e970043-a836-5520-b45d-454e88ad979a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3e25073-e8c8-572f-b7bb-7f9e85795377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151388} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d0402a4-a3a1-5aea-8065-8c452918edc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f66ee2a6-cdd7-5b19-895e-1ea6e9717b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80267" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "882b6ae6-35dc-576a-b270-e6c30d8dc303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350814} {:answer/id #uuid "5885f77f-1c1d-5617-ac43-f56402a4c8b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350815} {:answer/id #uuid "882891b9-d8b1-5881-b386-465e0094e042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350816} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6a3b7d-4cdc-5f1a-921c-bcf2726f13a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350817} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b50982-951f-5bfb-b583-6c22bae6ad96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e6a3b7d-4cdc-5f1a-921c-bcf2726f13a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350817} :answer-field/id #uuid "89264532-428b-52e8-a27f-a6938b52ab30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1caccc0-43ad-5e33-9901-fea327fe1e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-86898" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065540/assets/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
